The idea would be to prevent you from blessed-nuking every single
dangerous thing in the game as soon as they get to the castle and
wish for a magic marker, resulting in a boring-but-safe Gehennom
experience.  Don't like mind flayers and master mind flayers?
Nuke h from orbit.  Don't like eels?  Nuke ;.  Don't like master
and arch-liches?  Nuke L.  Don't like golden nagas?  Nuke N.
Afraid of minotaurs?  Make H go away (except for Titans, which
are fairly rare).  Et cetera.  Making success rate decay with the
number of _species_ that have been genocided, rather than the
number of scrolls that have been used, will encourage more
judicious use of class genocides.  Among other things, it would
provide incentive to expend two uncursed scrolls on mind flayers,
rather than blessed-nuking all humanoids.  It also increases the
odds that Gehennom will have at least a few "interesting" (i.e.,
potentially dangerous) monsters left in it.  The player can still
nuke the things he really can't stand, but he may have to pick
and choose *which* things he really can't stand, rather than just
mindlessly killing off anything even vaguely threatening.

The real beauty is, it has virtually no impact on the early game.
Prior to the castle, the user is unlikely to get enough genocide
opportunities for the change to even be noticed.  So the hard
part of the game doesn't get harder, but the boring part gets
more interesting.  It's win-win.


